-- Guess the City goes from 7 levels to 20.
--
-- The category shipped with 70 questions — ten per level, seven levels — and a
-- player who knows their landmarks reaches the end of it in an evening.
-- total_levels is floor(thinnest-language count / 10), so the only way to add a
-- level is to add ten more questions in EVERY language, not just in English.
--
-- This adds 130 cities x 7 languages = 910 rows, taking each language from 70
-- to 200 questions and the category from 7 levels to 20.
--
-- How the 130 were chosen:
--
--   * A city qualifies on its LANDMARK, not its skyline. The existing 70 are
--     the Eiffel Tower, the Colosseum, Hagia Sophia; a photo of glass towers at
--     dusk is unanswerable no matter which city it is. 143 candidate photos
--     were pulled from the lead image of each landmark's Wikipedia article and
--     every one of them was looked at; 13 were dropped as unrecognisable
--     (generic skylines, a black night shot, two logos, one collage) and
--     replaced from a second pass.
--   * Names come from Wikidata, which is where the app's other localised
--     content comes from, so all seven languages agree on the same place.
--     Brasilia's Georgian label is the country's name, so it carries an
--     explicit "(city)"; Panama City has the same collision in three languages
--     at once and was dropped rather than fudged.
--   * The three wrong answers are cities from the same region, so the question
--     is about the picture rather than about which continent the names are on.
--   * Levels 8-12 are the cities most people can place, 13-17 the ones that
--     need a traveller, 18-20 the ones that need someone who has been there.
--
-- The images are hotlinked Wikimedia Commons thumbnails, the same as the
-- original 70.

INSERT INTO public.questions
  (id, category_id, question_text, correct_answer, incorrect_answers,
